pictures and headlines from the war in  ukraine are flooding social media and  news outlets and many parents are  concerned about how their children are  processing everything we spoke with four  parents whose children rage in age for  age from five to twenty years old they  told us about the conversations they've  had with their kids and what worries  them the most  as a former social studies teacher i  want my girls to know what's going on in  the world i want them to pay attention  and i want them to care but certainly  not at the risk of their mental health  you want to make sure that  even the stress that you have as a  parent um doesn't necessarily  get related to your children when they  do not ask  that's when you start to wonder what do  you know what do you not know what  questions you have what are you worried  about what are you thinking to get a lot  of information from you know having  conversations with their peers and what  their peers related information from  what they see on social media so for me  my 12 year old one of the first things  he asked me was if this was going to  turn into world war iii and i don't i  don't know what do i say i'm worried too  how do we  make them feel safe while also  validating that there's something really  awful happening in the world and there's  a lot of kids and families who are  struggling  clinical psychologist cynthia martin of  the child mind institute joins us now to  share her advice on how to navigate  these tough conversations good morning  and let's start right there how do you  have this conversation while  still making children feel safe right  now absolutely it's an important  question and one of the pieces to first  recognize is that this is a big topic  and these are hard conversations for  parents to navigate with their children  and so first and foremost we want to  think about putting parents in the  driver's seat so parents are the ones  who are going to have the best control  over the informati