ed on the  child's age so teens especially they  they're not getting most of their the  information from their parents they're  getting information exactly exactly  exactly and most parents aren't as into  it as their kids are or even if you  think you're into it you're probably not  included in the same way your children  are right so you want to make sure that  you're having that you have some  oversight and some control over your  child's social media use as well as  really opening up the  stage for those conversations so that  your child knows if they heard something  or saw something on social media maybe  even when they weren't supposed to be on  it  they can come to their parent and talk  about that so dr martin i thought this  was a really interesting question we got  from one mom her name is theodora lau  she wanted to know is too much  information a bad thing is it okay to  hold back certain details especially  when you see some of the horrific images  that are coming out even the front page  of the new york times a couple of days  ago had that stark photo uh taken by  lindsay dario that showed  what's happening in ukraine is that too  much for kids yeah absolutely we do want  to limit  limit and control what a child is  exposed to and we want to do that based  on the child's age it needs to be  developmentally appropriate so there are  a lot of news outlets that are  appropriate for children and  present things in a factual but more  developmentally appropriate way and  leave out some of the details that are  going to be too upsetting for a child to  be able to process because we have to  keep in mind that our kids even though  some kids come across as very mature and  very eloquent in the way they speak and  logically they're saying a lot of things  that make sense just where they are in  terms of their development and their  brain development there's going to be a  discrepancy between what they can ask  and logically try to explain and where  their emot